    "proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2).  The section S23 has Smith invariants 1^22,3.  For a primitive r=(x,y), reflection integrality gives q(r)|2I(r), where I(r)=(a*x,(y,S23)).  Local unimodularity and primitivity exclude every prime away from (pi) and (3).  At (3), positive q-valuation puts x in (3) and every coefficient of y in the one-dimensional pairing radical modulo 3.  That radical is primitive and the discriminant has exponent one, so I(r) has valuation exactly one and v_3(q)<=1.  At (pi), if a is a unit then I(r) is a unit and v_pi(q)<=v_pi(2)=2.  If a=pi and y has a unit pairing, the same bound holds.  Otherwise nondegeneracy of S23 modulo pi gives y in pi*S23; primitivity makes x a unit and q=-pi*x^2 modulo pi^2, so v_pi(q)=1.  Thus v_pi(q) is 0,1,or 2 and v_3(q) is 0 or 1.  Z[sqrt(2)] has class number one and its totally positive units are squares.  The complete canonical list is therefore 1,2+pi,2,3,6+3pi,6.  In the final three classes both silver coefficients of y lie in the mod-three pairing radical.",
    "three_gate_radical_exclusion": "In the displayed section basis, ker(G mod 3) is generated by the last coordinate, whose raw vector is (-23,1^23), congruent to (1^24) mod 3. Thus every vector in the pairing radical has all raw coordinates congruent mod 3. Raw norm 3 means square sum 24: residue 0 is impossible, while residue +/-1 forces all coordinates +/-1 with one common sign, contradicting coordinate sum zero. Raw norms 4 and 5 have square sums 32 and 40. In a nonzero residue class, common odd parity makes every coordinate equal to the least representative +/-1 unless the square sum is already at least 48, contradicting sum zero; common even parity has square sum at least 96. Residue zero is impossible modulo 9. For raw norm 6, square sum 48. Residue 0 is again impossible. Writing a residue-1 coordinate as 1+3k, the zero-sum and square-sum equations give sum(k)=-8 and sum(k^2)=8, hence exactly eight coordinates are -2 and sixteen are 1; this violates the common coordinate parity of every odd-Leech vector. Residue -1 is the negative case. Therefore the radical has no vector of norm at most 6 (in particular no norm 3 or 6 vector)."
  },
